Grouped mutate() and filter() compile aggregates and window functions to OVER (PARTITION BY ...). Window functions need an order, so call arrange() first or pass order_by:.
A verb given a PHP closure runs the query so far, loads the rows, and continues in memory, returning a DataFrame. Expressions with no faithful SQL translation do the same. They include round() (R rounds halves to even), first(), last(), nth(), iqr(), cumprod(), consecutive_id(), and median() or quantile() outside PostgreSQL.

phplyr’s verbs and helpers are namespaced functions. Import the ones a file uses with use function:
use Phibble\DataFrame;
use function Phplyr\{phplyr, filter, mutate, group_by, summarise, arrange, desc, n, mean};
A method chain only needs phplyr() and the helpers it uses, such as n, mean, and desc. The verbs are its methods.
Three phplyr functions share names with PHP built-ins: count, min, and max. In a file that imports them, a bare count($array) calls phplyr’s count(). Write \count(), \min(), and \max() for the built-ins.

phplyr’s verbs and helpers are namespaced functions. Import the ones a file uses with use function:
use Phibble\DataFrame;
use function Phplyr\{phplyr, filter, mutate, group_by, summarise, arrange, desc, n, mean};
A method chain only needs phplyr() and the helpers it uses, such as n, mean, and desc. The verbs are its methods.
Three phplyr functions share names with PHP built-ins: count, min, and max. In a file that imports them, a bare count($array) calls phplyr’s count(). Write \count(), \min(), and \max() for the built-ins.

Loading data
phibble reads CSV files, PDO queries, and query builders, and builds frames from PHP arrays:
$schools = DataFrame::fromCsv('schools.csv');
$rows = DataFrame::fromRows([['id' =&gt; 1, 'x' =&gt; 2.5], ['id' =&gt; 2, 'x' =&gt; null]]);
$cols = DataFrame::fromColumns(['id' =&gt; [1, 2], 'x' =&gt; [2.5, null]]);
null is NA. DataFrames are immutable, and every verb returns a new frame. echo $frame prints the first rows in tibble’s format.

Row closures and vector helpers
phplyr accepts two kinds of expressions.
A row closure is an ordinary PHP closure. It receives the row as a Phibble\Row (read columns as $r-&gt;score or $r['score']) and, as a second argument, the table of the row’s group:
mutate(gap: fn ($r) =&gt; $r-&gt;score - $r-&gt;prior_score)
filter(fn ($r, $group) =&gt; $r-&gt;score &gt; mean('score')($group))
A vector helper is one of phplyr’s functions, such as lag('x'), cumsum('x'), min_rank('x'), or mean('x'). It is evaluated once per group on whole columns, with dplyr’s semantics. A summary helper used in mutate() returns one value per group, which is recycled to every row of the group:

Method chain (PHP 8.3+)Pipe (PHP 8.5)


$schools = phplyr(DataFrame::fromCsv('schools.csv'))-&gt;group_by('school')-&gt;get();

echo json_encode([
    'groups' =&gt; phplyr($schools)-&gt;group_vars(),
    'n_groups' =&gt; phplyr($schools)-&gt;n_groups(),
    'sizes' =&gt; \array_slice(phplyr($schools)-&gt;group_size(), 0, 4),
]), "\n\n";

// Grouped mutate: helpers are evaluated within each group.
echo phplyr($schools)
    -&gt;mutate(centered: fn ($r, $g) =&gt; round($r-&gt;score - mean('score')($g), 2), rank_in_school: min_rank(desc('score')))
    -&gt;ungroup()
    -&gt;head(4);

// One table per group.
$parts = phplyr($schools)-&gt;group_split();
echo "\n", \count($parts), ' tables; the first has ', $parts[0]-&gt;nrow(), " rows\n";


$schools = DataFrame::fromCsv('schools.csv') |&gt; group_by('school');

echo json_encode([
    'groups' =&gt; $schools |&gt; group_vars(),
    'n_groups' =&gt; $schools |&gt; n_groups(),
    'sizes' =&gt; \array_slice($schools |&gt; group_size(), 0, 4),
]), "\n\n";

// Grouped mutate: helpers are evaluated within each group.
echo $schools
    |&gt; mutate(centered: fn ($r, $g) =&gt; round($r-&gt;score - mean('score')($g), 2), rank_in_school: min_rank(desc('score')))
    |&gt; ungroup()
    |&gt; head(4);

// One table per group.
$parts = $schools |&gt; group_split();
echo "\n", \count($parts), ' tables; the first has ', $parts[0]-&gt;nrow(), " rows\n";



