Pivoting
pivot_longer() turns columns into rows of names and values. names_prefix strips a regular expression from the start of each name. names_sep or names_pattern split names into several names_to columns, and the special name '.value' takes the value column’s name from that part. values_drop_na: true drops rows whose values are all NA. names_transform and values_transform take a closure, or an array of closures by column. cols_vary: 'slowest' keeps each source column’s rows together.
pivot_wider() spreads names and values into columns. id_cols identify the rows (by default every other column). values_fill fills missing cells, values_fn combines duplicate cells, and names_prefix, names_sep, names_glue, and names_sort control the new names. Without values_fn, duplicate cells turn the value column into list-columns and raise tidyr’s warning. names_vary: 'slowest' groups each name’s value columns together. names_expand and id_expand add every combination of the names_from and id_cols values (sorted, factors by level). unused_fn summarises the columns that are in none of id_cols, names_from, and values_from.
Both verbs build tidyr’s pivot spec in phplyr and pass it to the table’s pivotLongerSpec() or pivotWiderSpec(), so a backend can run the pivot natively. build_longer_spec() and build_wider_spec() return that spec as a table, and pivot_longer_spec() and pivot_wider_spec() run an edited one. The superseded gather() and spread() are also available.

Method chain (PHP 8.3+)Pipe (PHP 8.5)


$scores = DataFrame::fromRows([
    ['student' =&gt; 'ann', 'wk1' =&gt; 10, 'wk2' =&gt; null, 'wk3' =&gt; 30],
    ['student' =&gt; 'bob', 'wk1' =&gt; 7, 'wk2' =&gt; 8, 'wk3' =&gt; 9],
]);

$long = phplyr($scores)-&gt;pivot_longer(starts_with('wk'), names_to: 'week', names_prefix: 'wk', values_to: 'points', values_drop_na: true)-&gt;get();
echo $long;

echo "\n", phplyr($long)-&gt;pivot_wider(names_from: 'week', values_from: 'points', names_prefix: 'week_', values_fill: 0, names_sort: true);


