The join verbs take the right-hand table as their first argument. The input table is the left-hand table x.



Function
Rows kept




inner_join($y)
Rows of x with a match in y


left_join($y)
All rows of x


right_join($y)
All rows of y


full_join($y)
All rows of both


semi_join($y)
Rows of x with a match, x’s columns only


anti_join($y)
Rows of x without a match


cross_join($y)
Every combination



by: names the key columns. A string or list joins on columns with the same name in both tables, and an associative array maps differing names: by: ['school' =&gt; 'school_id']. The forms mix, as in ['id', 'code' =&gt; 'country_code']. Without by:, the tables join on every column they share.

